{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3109","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3109","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3109\",\"parent_revision\":\"14a6c10e27a7c7f643c91da3e2f69235ca6c9551\",\"root_revision\":\"0dffe597a20d9e8a541ae5fbeb4ea417235441724f7294d9a051710b9ea2909a\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: Ideas\\ntype: Feature\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-21\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\nThis is an exploration/experiment item \u2014 its deliverable is an experiment result or design decision with evidence (a prototype on a real plan, a measured comparison, or a written design verdict), not a mandate to ship a feature.\\n\\nOrigin: mechanism mined from Claude Code's built-in ultracode Workflow orchestration system \u2014 a code-executed multi-agent workflow runtime whose design contract contains several simple-but-effective patterns DH can adopt without adopting the runtime itself.\\n\\nShared design constraint (same as #3097\u2013#3103): the model keeps the judgment but writes it down once, as typed data, at planning time; the mechanical consequence is applied deterministically by machinery that already exists. No new scheduler, no generic graph runtime \u2014 out of scope.\\n\\n## Acceptance Criteria\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- [ ] Work matches description\\n- [ ] Plan or implementation complete\\n</div>\\n\\n## Story\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nAs a **developer using Claude Code skills**, I want to **explore token/cost budgets as first-class plan data with hard dispatch ceilings** so that **the tooling becomes more capable and complete**.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: ultracode Workflow orchestration system\\n- **Priority**: Ideas\\n- **Added**: 2026-08-21\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\\n## What Why\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z-0</sub>\\n\\nBorrowed from ultracode: its runtime exposes budget.total / budget.spent() / budget.remaining() to the orchestration script, the target is a HARD ceiling (further agent dispatches throw once spent reaches total, not advisory), the pool is shared across the whole run rather than per-agent, and fan-out can be scaled to budget statically (fleet size = budget / expected-cost-per-agent) or dynamically (loop while remaining > reserve). DH has no cost governance at any level: the #3060 incident spent ~$100 with no mechanism aware it was happening, and nothing today would stop a recurrence at $50, $100, or $500. Complexity scoring (#3097) makes spend proportional per task; this item bounds TOTAL spend per plan/milestone and makes the bound enforceable.\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z-1</sub>\\n\\nBorrowed from ultracode: its runtime exposes budget.total / budget.spent() / budget.remaining() to the orchestration script, the target is a HARD ceiling (further agent dispatches throw once spent reaches total, not advisory), the pool is shared across the whole run rather than per-agent, and fan-out can be scaled to budget statically (fleet size = budget / expected-cost-per-agent) or dynamically (loop while remaining > reserve). DH has no cost governance at any level: the #3060 incident spent ~$100 with no mechanism aware it was happening, and nothing today would stop a recurrence at $50, $100, or $500. Complexity scoring (#3097) makes spend proportional per task; this item bounds TOTAL spend per plan/milestone and makes the bound enforceable.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Mitigates\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\nUnbounded plan/milestone spend (the #3060 incident cost dimension \u2014 recursive dispatch compounding with no ceiling); invisible spend (no queryable spent-so-far during a run); fan-out sized by habit instead of by budget.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Benefit Lands In\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z-0</sub>\\n\\nThe dispatch loop (checks remaining budget before each dispatch, refuses past the ceiling and escalates to the orchestrator instead \u2014 composes with #3099's escalate policy), planning (plan carries a budget; the planner sizes decomposition and parallelism to it), and user trust (a stated bound the system actually honors).\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z-1</sub>\\n\\nThe dispatch loop (checks remaining budget before each dispatch, refuses past the ceiling and escalates to the orchestrator instead \u2014 composes with #3099's escalate policy), planning (plan carries a budget; the planner sizes decomposition and parallelism to it), and user trust (a stated bound the system actually honors).\\n</div>\\n\\n## Proposed Exploration\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z-0</sub>\\n\\n1. Decide the unit (tokens are measurable per-dispatch in Claude Code; dollars derive from them) and where the ledger lives (plan-level field in sam_schema, incremented by the dispatch path).\\n2. Budget exhaustion is an escalation event, never silent truncation of remaining work \u2014 the orchestrator/human decides top-up vs stop, and the refusal must state what work remains (per this repo's no-silent-caps rules).\\n3. Explore budget-aware fan-out: batch width chosen from remaining budget and per-task expected cost (from #3097's score).\\n4. Tabletop-replay the #3060 incident against a plan budget: confirm the cascade would have hit the ceiling and escalated long before ~$100.\\n</div>\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z-1</sub>\\n\\n1. Decide the unit (tokens are measurable per-dispatch in Claude Code; dollars derive from them) and where the ledger lives (plan-level field in sam_schema, incremented by the dispatch path).\\n2. Budget exhaustion is an escalation event, never silent truncation of remaining work \u2014 the orchestrator/human decides top-up vs stop, and the refusal must state what work remains (per this repo's no-silent-caps rules).\\n3. Explore budget-aware fan-out: batch width chosen from remaining budget and per-task expected cost (from #3097's score).\\n4. Tabletop-replay the #3060 incident against a plan budget: confirm the cascade would have hit the ceiling and escalated long before ~$100.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Related\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n#3097 (per-task expected cost feeds the ledger forecast); #3099 (exhaustion escalates via the same policy machinery); #3102 (same counters-as-data mechanism at plan scope).\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"24ae5c9f828bfd209c6b8d4e1c88efde3bc7bfa9983627cb61affe7e84689c0f\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABQIqS8w\"}"}